      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for starting that. It's a decent summary, but honestly, it feels a bit thin for a \"thorough\" study guide. It's quite short, so the length score is definitely on the low side.\n\nCan you expand on this? I need you to go much deeper into the mathematical implications and the specific setup of the EPR paradox that leads into Bell's work. Try to be as exhaustive as possible with the derivation and the conceptual nuances so the guide is actually substantive. Again, please don't just repeat yourself or use filler to get the length up—I need actual technical depth.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Bell's Theorem, formulated by John Bell in 1964, is a pivotal result in quantum mechanics that challenges the classical notion of local realism. Local realism is the idea that physical properties of particles are determined by their local environment and that information cannot travel faster than the speed of light. Bell's Theorem demonstrates that no local hidden variable theory can reproduce all the predictions of quantum mechanics.\n\nBell's Theorem is based on the concept of Bell inequalities, which are mathematical inequalities that any local hidden variable theory must satisfy. Quantum mechanics, however, predicts violations of these inequalities in certain experiments. Bell's Theorem shows that if quantum mechanics is correct, then the results of experiments involving entangled particles will violate Bell inequalities, thus ruling out local hidden variable theories.\n\nEntanglement is a quantum phenomenon where particles become correlated in such a way that the state of one particle is dependent on the state of another, regardless of the distance between them. When two particles are entangled, a measurement on one particle instantly determines the state of the other particle, even if they are light-years apart. This instantaneous correlation seems to violate the principle of locality, which states that objects are only influenced by their immediate surroundings.\n\nBell's Theorem has been tested in numerous experiments",
      "source": "target",
